>
> **Task 5 requires a physical Android tablet.** Everything through Task 4 can run unattended; Task 5 needs a human to install the APK on real hardware and follow a timed manual protocol — it cannot be delegated to a subagent.

**Goal:** Prove (or disprove) that a Capacitor-wrapped Android app can reliably keep receiving GPS location fixes while backgrounded/screen-locked, before the Slice A backend's ping-ingestion contract is finalized — this is PLAN 18.0 (structural amendments) plus the first half of the tablet-client work implied by PLAN 18.1 / design-spec §8.

**Architecture:** A new top-level `tablet/` Capacitor + React + TypeScript app (Android only), using `@capacitor-community/background-geolocation` for background-capable GPS watching. A minimal spike screen starts/stops a watcher and persists every received fix to `localStorage` (survives app kill/reopen) so a human can background the app, wait, then reopen and see what arrived.

**Tech Stack:** Capacitor 7 (`@capacitor/core`, `@capacitor/android`, `@capacitor/cli`), React 18 + TypeScript, Vite, Tailwind v4 (`@tailwindcss/vite`) — matching Atlas's existing frontend versions exactly, `@capacitor-community/background-geolocation` v1.x, Vitest + Testing Library for unit tests, npm.

